Topological Coherence and Stability (TCST)

ouadi Maakoul + chatGpt+ Deepseek+ Gemini+ Claude 

Abstract

We propose the Theory of Topological Coherence and Stability (TCST), a minimal falsifiable framework connecting local consistency to global dynamical stability in recursive cognitive systems. On a finite simplicial complex K with a cellular sheaf \mathcal{F} of stalks \mathbb{R}^d, we distinguish two types of obstruction: cohomological (H^1(K;\mathcal{F})\neq 0, arising when monodromy has a non-trivial fixed subspace) and spectral (\lambda_2(L_0)>0, arising when monodromy is fixed-point-free). We prove a Hodge decomposition lemma relating tension T(s)=\|\delta_0 s\|_2 to the physical component s_\perp, construct a constant-sheaf counter-example showing \Gamma(c)=0 and T(s)<\infty do not imply boundedness, restrict the floor theorem \inf_s\|c-\delta_0 s\|_2=\Gamma(c) to graphs, and adopt the annulus as a non-vacuous falsifier where triangle penalties and harmonic measures genuinely diverge. We rewrite the stability theorem as a mean-square bound on the coupled stochastic system and correct the nonlinear sheaf Laplacian gradient. Thermodynamic interpretability is framed via a friction-tensor metric with proper unit conventions. Three kill tests are specified.

