1. Introduction

1.1 The Theoretical Gap

The rapid empirical progress of large-scale neural architectures has outpaced the development of rigorous theoretical frameworks for recursive self-application. Hansen and Ghrist (2019) develop the spectral theory of cellular sheaves, lifting the graph Laplacian to the Hodge Laplacian and relating spectral data to sheaf cohomology and cell structure. The sheaf Laplacian becomes the graph Laplacian when the sheaf is trivial. Jiang, Lim, Yao, and Ye (2011) prove that every edge flow decomposes orthogonally into gradient, curl, and harmonic components, where the harmonic flow measures global cyclic inconsistency. The question of whether these obstructions control dynamical stability remains open.

1.2 Relation to Prior Work

Cellular sheaves. Hansen–Ghrist (2019) provide eigenvalue interlacing, sparsification, effective resistance, synchronization, and sheaf approximation for cellular sheaves. Anwer, Riess, and Hale (2026) show that the fundamental obstruction to recovering a nonlinear edge potential from trajectory data is topological, measured by sheaf cohomology; recovery is possible if and only if H^1(G;\mathcal{F})=0.

HodgeRank. The harmonic flow is locally acyclic but globally cyclic, providing information on whether inconsistency arises locally or globally. The cohomology group measures the obstruction to local solutions being global solutions.

Thermodynamic metrics. Sivak and Crooks (2012) derive a friction tensor inducing a Riemannian manifold on thermodynamic states, controlling dissipation of finite-time transformations within the linear-response regime. Excess work is bounded below by \mathcal{L}^2(\epsilon)/\tau, where \mathcal{L} is thermodynamic length.

Nonlinear sheaf diffusion. Hansen and Ghrist (2021) construct nonlinear sheaf Laplacians from edge potential functions, where the heat equation is gradient descent on a Dirichlet energy.

1.3 Scope

No claims about artificial general intelligence, consciousness, or the thermodynamic interpretation of information geometry. A mathematical framework whose components are separately falsifiable.

