Appendix B. Cellular Sheaves: Full Construction

B.1 Sheaf Axioms

A cellular sheaf \mathcal{F} on a simplicial complex K assigns:

· to each simplex \sigma\in K, a vector space \mathcal{F}(\sigma) (the stalk);
· to each incidence \sigma\trianglelefteq\tau (i.e., \sigma is a face of \tau), a linear restriction map \rho_{\sigma\trianglelefteq\tau}:\mathcal{F}(\sigma)\to\mathcal{F}(\tau);
  such that:

1. \rho_{\sigma\trianglelefteq\sigma}=\mathrm{id}_{\mathcal{F}(\sigma)} for all \sigma;
2. \rho_{\sigma\trianglelefteq\nu}=\rho_{\tau\trianglelefteq\nu}\circ\rho_{\sigma\trianglelefteq\tau} whenever \sigma\trianglelefteq\tau\trianglelefteq\nu.

For a cellular sheaf of vector spaces, the stalk is \mathbb{R}^d for some d, and the restriction maps are linear.

B.2 Orthogonal Restriction Maps

We restrict to restriction maps that are orthogonal matrices: \rho_{\sigma\trianglelefteq\tau}\in O(d). This ensures that the induced inner products on cochains are standard and that the Hodge Laplacian is well-behaved spectrally.

For the constant sheaf \underline{\mathbb{R}}^d, all restriction maps are the identity: \rho_{\sigma\trianglelefteq\tau}=I_d. This recovers the standard cochain complex with trivial coefficients.

B.3 Monodromy

Let \gamma=e_1e_2\cdots e_k be a closed path in the 1-skeleton (a cycle). Orient each edge e_i=(v_i^-,v_i^+). The monodromy around \gamma is

M_\gamma=\rho_{v_k^+\triangleleft e_k}\rho_{v_k^-\triangleleft e_k}^{-1}\circ\cdots\circ\rho_{v_1^+\triangleleft e_1}\rho_{v_1^-\triangleleft e_1}^{-1}\in O(d).

The monodromy is the holonomy of the connection defined by the restriction maps. For the constant sheaf, M_\gamma=I_d for all \gamma.

B.4 Coboundary Operator

The sheaf coboundary \delta_0:C^0(K;\mathcal{F})\to C^1(K;\mathcal{F}) is defined on a 0-cochain s=(s_v)_{v\in V} by

(\delta_0 s)_e=\rho_{v^+\triangleleft e}s_{v^+}-\rho_{v^-\triangleleft e}s_{v^-},

where e=(v^-,v^+) is an oriented edge. The higher coboundaries \delta_k:C^k\to C^{k+1} are defined analogously with alternating sums over face incidences.

B.5 Cohomology

The sheaf cohomology is

H^k(K;\mathcal{F})=\ker\delta_k/\operatorname{im}\delta_{k-1}.

For a connected complex, H^0(K;\mathcal{F})\cong\Gamma(K;\mathcal{F}), the space of global sections (elements s\in C^0 with \delta_0 s=0).

B.6 Sheaf Laplacian

The sheaf Laplacian on C^0 is

L_0=\delta_0^\top\delta_0:C^0(K;\mathcal{F})\to C^0(K;\mathcal{F}),

where \delta_0^\top is the adjoint with respect to the standard inner products. L_0 is symmetric positive semi-definite, and \ker L_0=\ker\delta_0=H^0(K;\mathcal{F}). The Fiedler value \lambda_2(L_0) is the smallest positive eigenvalue.

B.7 Reduction to Graph Laplacian

For the constant sheaf \underline{\mathbb{R}} on a connected graph G, L_0=\delta_0^\top\delta_0 is the standard graph Laplacian L_G=D-A. For \underline{\mathbb{R}}^k, L_0=L_G\otimes I_k. This is the sense in which the sheaf Laplacian generalizes the graph Laplacian.

B.8 Dimension Formula for Cycle Graphs

Let G=C_n be the cycle graph with n vertices and n edges. For a sheaf with stalks \mathbb{R}^d and monodromy M around the unique cycle:

· \dim C^0=\dim C^1=nd;
· \dim H^0=\dim\ker(M-I_d);
· \dim H^1=nd-\dim\operatorname{im}\delta_0=\dim\ker(M-I_d).

Thus H^0 and H^1 are isomorphic as vector spaces, and both vanish iff M has no non-trivial fixed vector (e.g., a rotation by an angle not a multiple of 2\pi in \mathbb{R}^2).

Consequence. For fixed-point-free monodromy, H^1=0: every 1-cochain is exact. The frustration is not cohomological but spectral: \lambda_2(L_0)>0 measures the cost of closing the cycle.

