Appendix C. Hodge Decomposition: Full Proofs

C.1 Lemma M0 (General Connected Complex)

Statement. Let K be connected with sheaf Laplacian L_0=\delta_0^\top\delta_0. Every s\in C^0(K;\mathcal{F}) decomposes uniquely as

s=s_\parallel+s_\perp,
\qquad
s_\parallel\in\ker L_0,
\qquad
s_\perp\in(\ker L_0)^\perp,

and

\|s_\perp\|_0\leq\frac{1}{\sqrt{\lambda_2(L_0)}}\|\delta_0 s\|_1=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\lambda_2(L_0)}}T(s),

with equality iff s_\perp lies in the \lambda_2(L_0)-eigenspace.

Proof. Since L_0 is symmetric positive semi-definite, the spectral theorem gives an orthonormal eigenbasis \{u_i\}_{i=1}^{N} with eigenvalues 0=\lambda_1<\lambda_2\leq\cdots\leq\lambda_N. The kernel is \ker L_0=\operatorname{span}\{u_1,\ldots,u_{m}\} where m=\dim H^0. Decompose s=\sum_i c_i u_i with c_i=\langle s,u_i\rangle. Then

s_\parallel=\sum_{i=1}^m c_i u_i,
\qquad
s_\perp=\sum_{i>m} c_i u_i,

and

\|\delta_0 s\|_1^2=\langle L_0 s,s\rangle=\sum_{i>m}\lambda_i c_i^2\geq\lambda_2(L_0)\sum_{i>m}c_i^2=\lambda_2(L_0)\|s_\perp\|_0^2.

Taking square roots gives the bound; equality iff c_i=0 for all \lambda_i>\lambda_2(L_0). \blacksquare

C.2 Lemma M0 on K_3 with Constant Sheaf

For K_3 with \underline{\mathbb{R}}, the coboundary is

\delta_0=\begin{pmatrix}-1&1&0\\0&-1&1\\1&0&-1\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
L_0=\delta_0^\top\delta_0=\begin{pmatrix}2&-1&-1\\-1&2&-1\\-1&-1&2\end{pmatrix}.

Characteristic polynomial: \det(L_0-\lambda I)=-\lambda(\lambda-3)^2. Eigenvalues 0,3,3. Eigenvector for \lambda=0: \mathbf{1}=(1,1,1)^\top/\sqrt{3}. Eigenspace for \lambda=3: \mathbf{1}^\perp. Hence

\|s_\perp\|_0=\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\|\delta_0 s\|_1.

C.3 Lemma M0 on C_n with Constant Sheaf

For the cycle graph C_n, eigenvalues of L_0 are

\lambda_k=2-2\cos(2\pi k/n),
\qquad
k=0,1,\ldots,n-1,

with \lambda_0=0 and

\lambda_2=2-2\cos(2\pi/n)\sim\frac{4\pi^2}{n^2}\quad(n\to\infty).

Thus the bound degrades as n^{-1}: \|s_\perp\|_0\lesssim n\,T(s)/(2\pi).

C.4 Floor Theorem for Graphs

Statement. Let K be a graph (so K_2=\emptyset, \delta_1=0, C^2=0). For every c\in C^1(K;\mathcal{F}),

\inf_{s\in C^0}\|c-\delta_0 s\|_1=\Gamma(c)=\|h\|_1,

where c=\delta_0 s_0+h is the Hodge decomposition of c.

Proof. Since K is a graph, C^2=0, so Z^1=C^1 and B^1=\operatorname{im}\delta_0. The Hodge Laplacian L_1=\delta_0\delta_0^\top. Its kernel is

\ker L_1=\ker(\delta_0^\top)=(\operatorname{im}\delta_0)^\perp,

where the first equality uses \delta_1=0. The Hodge decomposition C^1=\operatorname{im}\delta_0\oplus\ker L_1 is orthogonal. Write c=\delta_0 s_0+h with h\in\ker L_1. For any s\in C^0,

\|c-\delta_0 s\|_1^2=\|h+\delta_0(s_0-s)\|_1^2=\|h\|_1^2+\|\delta_0(s_0-s)\|_1^2\geq\|h\|_1^2,

with equality iff \delta_0(s_0-s)=0. Taking the infimum gives \Gamma(c)=\|h\|_1. \blacksquare

C.5 Corrected Floor for General Complexes

For a general simplicial complex with K_2\neq\emptyset, the Hodge decomposition is

C^1=\operatorname{im}\delta_0\oplus\ker L_1\oplus\operatorname{im}\delta_1^\top,

orthogonally. Write c=\delta_0 s_0+h+\delta_1^\top t. Then for any s\in C^0,

\|c-\delta_0 s\|_1^2=\|h\|_1^2+\|\delta_1^\top t\|_1^2+\|\delta_0(s_0-s)\|_1^2

by orthogonality, so

\inf_s\|c-\delta_0 s\|_1=\sqrt{\|h\|_1^2+\|\delta_1^\top t\|_1^2}=\|h+\delta_1^\top t\|_1.

The v3.0 floor theorem \inf_s\|c-\delta_0 s\|_1=\|h\|_1 is therefore false for complexes with 2-cells; the curl component \delta_1^\top t is an additional obstruction that local node adjustments cannot remove.

C.6 Generalized Hodge Decomposition

Statement. For each k, C^k(K;\mathcal{F})=\operatorname{im}\delta_{k-1}\oplus\ker L_k\oplus\operatorname{im}\delta_k^\top, and \ker L_k\cong H^k(K;\mathcal{F}).

Proof. Standard Hodge theory for finite-dimensional chain complexes with inner products (Eckmann 1944; Lim 2020). The key identities are \operatorname{im}\delta_{k-1}\perp\ker L_k\perp\operatorname{im}\delta_k^\top, which follow from \delta^2=0 and the definition of adjoints. \blacksquare

