Appendix E. Energy Landscapes and Hessian Analysis

E.1 Variant A (Uncoupled)

\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{unc}}(s)=\frac12 s^\top L_0 s+\frac{\mu}{2}\|P_\parallel s\|_0^2.

Gradient:

\nabla\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{unc}}(s)=L_0 s+\mu P_\parallel s.

Gradient descent with step \eta:

s_{t+1}=s_t-\eta(L_0 s_t+\mu P_\parallel s_t)=(I-\eta L_0-\eta\mu P_\parallel)s_t.

On \ker L_0: s_{\parallel,t+1}=(1-\eta\mu)s_{\parallel,t}. On (\ker L_0)^\perp: s_{\perp,t+1}=(I-\eta L_0)s_{\perp,t}, with spectral radius 1-\eta\lambda_2(L_0) on the lowest non-zero eigenspace. Stability: 0<\eta\mu<2 and 0<\eta\lambda_2(L_0)<2.

E.2 Variant B (Coupled)

\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{cpl}}(s)=\frac12 s^\top L_0 s+\frac{\mu}{2}\|P_\parallel s\|_0^2+\nu\,s_\parallel^\top A s_\perp.

Here A:\ker L_0\to(\ker L_0)^\perp is a linear map. The cross term is non-zero for A\neq 0.

Gradient. Using \nabla(s_\parallel^\top A s_\perp)=P_\parallel A s_\perp+P_\perp A^\top s_\parallel,

\nabla\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{cpl}}(s)=L_0 s+\mu P_\parallel s+\nu(P_\parallel A s_\perp+P_\perp A^\top s_\parallel).

Hessian. In block form with respect to C^0=\ker L_0\oplus(\ker L_0)^\perp:

H=\begin{pmatrix}\mu I & \nu A\\ \nu A^\top & L_0|_{(\ker L_0)^\perp}\end{pmatrix}.

Positive definiteness. By the Schur complement criterion, H\succ 0 iff L_0|_{(\ker L_0)^\perp}\succ 0 and

\mu I-\nu^2 A L_0^{-1}|_{(\ker L_0)^\perp}A^\top\succ 0.

For the constant sheaf on K_3, L_0|_{(\ker L_0)^\perp}=3I_2, so H\succ 0 iff \mu>(\nu^2/3)\|A\|_{\mathrm{op}}^2.

Derived contraction factors. With a=\|A\|_{\mathrm{op}} and s=s_\parallel+s_\perp, the gradient descent recurrences are

s_{\parallel,t+1}=(1-\eta\mu)s_{\parallel,t}-\eta\nu A s_{\perp,t},
\qquad
s_{\perp,t+1}=(1-\eta\lambda_2(L_0))s_{\perp,t}-\eta\nu A^\top s_{\parallel,t}.

Taking norms:

\|s_{\parallel,t+1}\|\leq\alpha\|s_{\parallel,t}\|+\Lambda\|s_{\perp,t}\|,
\qquad
\|s_{\perp,t+1}\|\leq\beta\|s_{\perp,t}\|+\Lambda\|s_{\parallel,t}\|,

with \alpha=1-\eta\mu, \beta=1-\eta\lambda_2(L_0), \Lambda=\eta\nu a.

Coupled stability. The block matrix M_s=I-\eta H has spectral radius <1 iff 0<\eta\lambda_i(H)<2 for all eigenvalues. The monotone contraction region is 0<\eta\lambda_{\max}(H)\leq 1.

E.3 Nonlinear Extension

For nonlinear U_v:\mathcal{F}(v)\to\mathbb{R}, the energy is

\mathcal{E}(s)=\frac12\|\delta_0 s\|_1^2+\sum_{v\in V}U_v(s_v)=\frac12 s^\top L_0 s+U(s),

where U(s)=\sum_v U_v(s_v). The gradient is

\nabla\mathcal{E}(s)=L_0 s+\nabla U(s),

with \nabla U(s)=(\nabla U_v(s_v))_{v\in V}. The v3.0 expression \delta_0^\top\circ\nabla U\circ\delta_0 is ill-typed because \delta_0:C^0\to C^1 and \nabla U:C^0\to C^0 cannot be composed as written. The correct gradient is the sum above.

Theorem M0-S applies to the linearization around a critical point s^* of \mathcal{E}: H=\nabla^2\mathcal{E}(s^*)=L_0+\nabla^2 U(s^*). Global stability for non-convex U is an open problem, addressed by standard Lyapunov theory for gradient flows with compact sublevel sets.

