Appendix F. Mean-Square Stability Theorem

F.1 Full Coupled System

State X_t=(s_t,h_t)\in C^0\oplus\mathcal{H}^1. Dynamics:

s_{t+1}=(I-\eta H)s_t+A_h h_t,
\qquad
h_{t+1}=(1-\kappa)h_t+\xi_t,
\qquad
\xi_t\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^2 I_{\dim\mathcal{H}^1}).

In block form:

X_{t+1}=\underbrace{\begin{pmatrix}I-\eta H & A_h\\ 0 & (1-\kappa)I\end{pmatrix}}_{=:M}X_t+\underbrace{\begin{pmatrix}0\\ I\end{pmatrix}\xi_t}_{=:B\xi_t}.

F.2 Spectral Radius of M

The eigenvalues of the block upper-triangular M are those of I-\eta H and those of (1-\kappa)I. Thus

\rho(M)=\max\bigl(\rho(I-\eta H),\,1-\kappa\bigr).

Under H2 (H\succ 0, \eta\lambda_{\max}(H)<2), \rho(I-\eta H)<1. Under H3 (\kappa\in(0,1]), 1-\kappa\in[0,1). Hence \rho(M)<1.

F.3 Discrete Lyapunov Equation

The stationary covariance \Sigma=\lim_{t\to\infty}\mathbb{E}[X_tX_t^\top] solves

\Sigma=M\Sigma M^\top+\sigma^2 BB^\top.

Since \rho(M)<1, the unique positive semi-definite solution is

\Sigma=\sigma^2\sum_{k=0}^\infty M^k BB^\top (M^\top)^k.

F.4 Mean-Square Bound

For any t,

\mathbb{E}[\|X_{t+1}\|^2]=\mathbb{E}[X_t^\top M^\top M X_t]+\sigma^2\mathrm{tr}(B^\top B)
\leq\rho(M)^2\mathbb{E}[\|X_t\|^2]+\sigma^2\dim\mathcal{H}^1.

By induction,

\mathbb{E}[\|X_t\|^2]\leq\rho(M)^{2t}\mathbb{E}[\|X_0\|^2]+\sigma^2\dim\mathcal{H}^1\frac{1-\rho(M)^{2t}}{1-\rho(M)^2}.

In the limit,

\boxed{\lim_{t\to\infty}\mathbb{E}[\|X_t\|^2]\leq\frac{\sigma^2\dim\mathcal{H}^1}{1-\rho(M)^2}.}

F.5 Component Bounds

Decompose \|X_t\|^2=\|s_t\|_0^2+\|h_t\|_1^2. For s:

\|s_t\|_0^2\leq\underbrace{\frac{T(s_t)^2}{\lambda_2(L_0)}}_{\text{from Lemma M0}}+\underbrace{\frac{\Lambda^2}{(1-\alpha)^2}\cdot\frac{T(s_t)^2}{\lambda_2(L_0)}}_{\text{gauge coupling}},

which, under the deterministic bound T(s_t)\leq\epsilon (which holds in the noiseless case and in mean-square for the noisy case), gives

\limsup_{t\to\infty}\mathbb{E}[\|s_t\|_0^2]\leq\frac{\epsilon^2}{\lambda_2(L_0)}\left(1+\frac{\Lambda^2}{(1-\alpha)^2}\right).

For h:

\lim_{t\to\infty}\mathbb{E}[\|h_t\|_1^2]=\frac{\sigma^2\dim\mathcal{H}^1}{\kappa(2-\kappa)}.

The two bounds combine via orthogonality of C^0 and C^1 in the direct sum.

F.6 Almost-Sure Unboundedness of \limsup

Gaussian AR(1) has \limsup_{t\to\infty}\|h_t\|=\infty almost surely (Law of Iterated Logarithm). Thus the deterministic bound \|h_t\|\leq C for all t is impossible; only the mean-square or in-probability bound is meaningful. This is why H1 was dropped in v4.0.

