Appendix H. Thermodynamic Framework

H.1 Friction Tensor

For a thermodynamic system with state z\in\mathcal{M} (a smooth manifold) and velocity \dot z, the dissipation potential \Phi(z,\dot z) is a non-negative function convex in \dot z, vanishing at \dot z=0. The friction tensor is

\zeta_{ij}(z)=\frac{\partial^2\Phi}{\partial\dot z^i\partial\dot z^j}\bigg|_{\dot z=0}.

For a quadratic dissipation potential \Phi=\frac12\dot z^\top\zeta\dot z, the friction tensor is constant. In general, \zeta depends on z and defines a Riemannian metric on \mathcal{M}.

H.2 Thermodynamic Length

For a protocol z:[0,\tau]\to\mathcal{M} with z(0)=z_i, z(\tau)=z_f, the thermodynamic length is

\mathcal{L}=\int_0^\tau\sqrt{\dot z(t)^\top\zeta(z(t))\dot z(t)}\,dt.

H.3 Finite-Time Excess Work

Within the linear-response regime (Sivak–Crooks 2012),

W_{\mathrm{ex}}\geq\frac{\mathcal{L}^2}{\tau}.

Quasi-static protocols (\tau\to\infty) achieve W_{\mathrm{ex}}\to 0; fast protocols cost more. The Landauer bound k_BT\ln 2 per erased bit is the quasi-static limit.

H.4 Landauer Bound in Nats

If S denotes differential entropy in nats, the Landauer bound is

Q\geq k_BT\,\Delta S.

If S is in bits, then Q\geq k_BT\ln 2\cdot\Delta S. We use nats throughout: Q_t\geq k_BT\max(0,S_t-S_{t+1}).

H.5 Stationary-State Cost

Under Theorem M0-S, the covariance \Sigma_t\to\Sigma_\infty. The differential entropy S_t=\frac12\log\det(2\pi e\Sigma_t) converges. The total stabilization cost is

Q_{\mathrm{total}}\geq k_BT\max(0,S_0-S_\infty).

The gauge-dissipation rate \mu controls the rate of entropy reduction, hence the total cost.

H.6 Fisher–Rao Connection

On the space of positive-definite covariance matrices, the Fisher–Rao metric is

g_{\mathrm{FR}}(\delta\Sigma,\delta\Sigma)=\frac12\mathrm{tr}(\Sigma^{-1}\delta\Sigma\,\Sigma^{-1}\delta\Sigma).

For a Gaussian family, this is (up to a factor) the affine-invariant metric. The friction tensor for quasi-static isothermal processes with Gaussian fluctuations coincides with the Fisher–Rao metric in the linear-response regime. This gives a coordinate-independent meaning to \Delta S.

H.7 Coordinate Dependence

Differential entropy is not invariant under general coordinate transformations on \mathcal{M}. Only when the coordinates are physically meaningful (e.g., derived from \zeta) does \Delta S represent a physical heat exchange. This is why the friction-tensor construction is necessary: it selects a preferred geometry.

