Appendix L. Open Problems

1. Global stability for non-convex U. Theorem M0-S applies to the linearization. Global stability of the nonlinear sheaf diffusion remains open for general non-convex potentials.
2. Explicit friction tensor for sheaf diffusion. The friction tensor \zeta_{ij} is defined abstractly; an explicit derivation from a physical dissipation potential \Phi for the sheaf diffusion model is future work.
3. Cohomological vs spectral obstruction classification. A complete classification of which monodromies produce cohomological vs spectral frustration, and the dynamical consequences of each, is open.
4. Tightness of the mean-square bound. The bound \rho(M)^2 may be loose; the actual decay rate could be sharper.
5. Higher-dimensional cohomology. The framework currently focuses on H^1; extension to H^k for k\geq 2 requires higher-dimensional sheaf Laplacians.
6. Non-orthogonal restriction maps. The restriction maps are assumed orthogonal; relaxation to general linear maps changes the spectral theory and is open.
7. Experimental validation. All predictions are theoretical; empirical validation on neural or cognitive systems is future work.

